\use{book}
\use{chords}

\title{First Chords}
\author{A Beginner's Guide to the Guitar}
\date{}

\frontmatter
\maketitle

\chapter{Preface}

This little book teaches the handful of chords that almost every song a beginner wants
to play is built from. There is no music theory to wade through and nothing to memorise
in advance: each chord is shown as a diagram you can copy straight onto the fretboard,
with the fingers numbered so you know exactly where each one goes.

Work through it slowly. Learn one chord, play it until it rings cleanly, and only then
move on to the next. By the end you will know enough chords to play hundreds of songs.

\mainmatter

\chapter{Reading a Chord Box}

A chord diagram is a picture of the top of the guitar neck as you look down at it while
playing. The six vertical lines are the strings, from the lowest and thickest on the
left to the highest and thinnest on the right. The horizontal lines are the metal
frets, and the thick line across the top is the nut, where the fretboard ends.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord{C}{x 3 2 0 1 0}{. 3 2 . 1 .}}
\bigskip

A black dot shows where to press a string down, and the number inside it is the finger
to use: \textbf{1} is the index finger, \textbf{2} the middle, \textbf{3} the ring
finger, and \textbf{4} the little finger. Above the nut, an ``o'' marks a string you
play \emph{open} (without pressing it at all), and an ``×'' marks a string you do not
play. The diagram above is a C major chord: press the fifth string at the third fret,
the fourth at the second, the second at the first, and strum from the fifth string
down.

\chapter{Your First Chord}

The friendliest chord to start with is E minor. It uses only two fingers, and every
other string rings open, so it is almost impossible to get wrong.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord{Em}{0 2 2 0 0 0}{. 2 3 . . .}}
\bigskip

Place your middle and ring fingers on the fifth and fourth strings at the second fret,
press firmly just behind the fret, and strum all six strings. If any string buzzes,
press a little harder or move your finger closer to the fret. Play it again and again
until the chord sounds clear every time.

\section{Adding a Finger: E Major}

Slide back to E major by adding your index finger to the third string at the first
fret. The shape of the other two fingers does not change at all.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord{E}{0 2 2 1 0 0}{. 2 3 1 . .}}
\bigskip

\chapter{Two More Open Chords}

A major and D major join E minor to make a trio that already covers a great many songs.
A major packs three fingers onto the second fret.

\bigskip
\centerline{
  \chord{A}{x 0 2 2 2 0}{. . 1 2 3 .}\hskip 30
  \chord{D}{x x 0 2 3 2}{. . . 1 3 2}
}
\bigskip

For A major, do not play the lowest string — the ``×'' reminds you to start your strum
from the fifth string. For D major, play only the top four strings. D is the first
chord that asks your fingers to sit close together, so take your time and check that
each note rings.

\chapter{Changing Chords}

Knowing a chord is only half the job; the other half is changing between chords without
stopping. The secret is to move all your fingers at once, as a single shape, rather
than placing them one at a time.

Pick any two chords you know — say E minor and A major — and practise switching back and
forth slowly, in time, even if there is a gap between them at first. Keep a steady, slow
count and let the changes catch up to it. Speed comes on its own once the movement is
smooth.

\section{The Next Mountain: F}

Sooner or later every guitarist meets the F chord, where one finger lies flat across all
six strings at the first fret. This is called a barre, drawn here as a thick bar.

\bigskip
\centerline{\chord[1 1 6]{F}{1 3 3 2 1 1}{1 3 4 2 1 1}}
\bigskip

It is hard at first and that is completely normal. Roll your index finger slightly onto
its side so the bony edge presses the strings, keep your thumb low on the back of the
neck, and be patient. When F rings out cleanly, you have crossed the threshold from
beginner to player.

\backmatter

\chapter{A Note on Practice}

Five focused minutes a day will take you further than an unfocused hour once a week.
Keep this book by your guitar, pick one chord or one change to work on each day, and
above all, play music you enjoy — that is what turns practice into playing.
